# Telegram Message Processing

Lux provides message-processing helpers for Telegram bots that need to route
updates, parse commands, preserve thread context, escape output, and handle
callback/deep-link payloads.

## Update Routing

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.Telegram.MessageProcessor

case MessageProcessor.classify_update(update) do
  :text_message -> handle_text(update["message"])
  :callback_query -> handle_callback(update["callback_query"])
  :inline_query -> handle_inline(update["inline_query"])
  :unknown -> :ignore
end
```

## Command Parsing

```elixir
{:ok, command} =
  MessageProcessor.parse_command("/deploy prod now", %{
    bot_username: "LuxBot"
  })

command.command
command.args
```

Group commands that mention another bot are ignored:

```elixir
MessageProcessor.parse_command("/deploy@OtherBot prod", %{bot_username: "LuxBot"})
#=> :ignore
```

## Deep Links

Telegram deep links arrive as `/start <payload>`:

```elixir
MessageProcessor.deep_link_payload("/start invite_123")
#=> {:ok, "invite_123"}
```

## Callback Queries

Use a simple `action:payload` convention for inline keyboard callbacks:

```elixir
MessageProcessor.decode_callback_data("approve:ticket-123")
#=> %{action: "approve", payload: "ticket-123"}
```

## Threads and Replies

```elixir
context = MessageProcessor.thread_context(message)

context.chat_id
context.message_thread_id
context.reply_to_message_id
```

The context map can be passed into response prisms so replies stay attached to
the correct chat, topic, and parent message.

## Formatting

Escape generated text before sending it with Telegram parse modes:

```elixir
MessageProcessor.escape_markdown_v2("hello_world!")
MessageProcessor.escape_html("<b>Tom & Co</b>")
```

## Notes

- Parse commands before handing messages to agent workflows.
- Preserve `message_thread_id` for forum topics.
- Treat callback data as routing metadata, not trusted authorization.
- Escape model-generated text before using MarkdownV2 or HTML parse modes.
